# Answer

**SES (socioeconomic status).** Its effect on BA degree completion collapses once academic
characteristics are added to the model that already contains SES.

## Reproduction (logistic regression on BA DEGREE COMPLETED, N=8,773, 236 events)
| specification | SES coef (unstd.) | SE | p |
|---|---|---|---|
| SES block only | **0.4729** | 0.0826 | <1e-24 |
| SES + academic characteristics (PERCENTILE IN CLASS) | **0.0814** | 0.0912 | 0.372 |
| SES + FEMALE -> + PERCENTILE IN CLASS | 0.4760 -> 0.0798 | 0.0913 | 0.383 |
Drop = 82.8% (83.2% with the sex dummy); HC1-robust SEs: p = 0.390 / 0.401. Quoted: 0.5024 -> 0.0923.
Odds ratio: OR 1.60 (95% CI 1.37-1.89) -> OR 1.08 (0.91-1.30). Pseudo-R2 0.014 -> 0.119.

## Why not an academic variable (mirror check)
PERCENTILE and ABILITY coefficients are essentially unmoved by adding SES
(|change| <= 1.6%; p < 1e-5 throughout, in every family and scaling). The collapse is
one-directional: it belongs to SES.

## Decision rule and ranking (% drop, A = SES+race+sex -> B = A + class percentile)
| variable | logit | LPM | probit | significance flip in all 9 family x scaling cells |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **SES** | **98.7%** | **93.7%** | **90.2%** | **yes (9/9)** |
| BLACK | 31.3% | 47.1% | 32.1% | no |
| HISPANIC | 27.2% | 35.6% | 22.4% | no (OLS only) |
| FEMALE | 77.3% | 86.3% | 80.1% | no (pA=0.088, never significant) |
FAMILY SIZE never drops (7-27%) and stays p<1e-16.

Scaling invariance (verified numerically): %drop and p-values are identical for raw, z and
0-1 scalings (t = 0.8922, p = 0.3723 in all three) - only absolute magnitudes depend on scale,
so the identity of the variable is scale-free while the 4-decimal match is not.

## Caveats
* Only the class-rank academic measure reproduces a *positive* ~0.09. Adding ABILITY as well
  (r = 0.699 with percentile) pushes SES to -0.2171 (p = 0.028); the ~0.09 value corresponds to
  adjustment by class rank specifically.
* 236 events (2.69% completion): EPV 78.7 for the headline model, 29.5 for the 7-predictor model;
  low completion rate suggests a right-censored outcome.
* The "curriculum" measure promised by the data dictionary is absent from the CSV.
* Race dummies are constant within race subsamples -> singular Hessian (flagged, not silently dropped).
